{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XEP9bLFfnmQWYF56WRawc5","type":"tool_result","content":"935\t# producing a file `tomllib` refuses to read back.\n936\t_toml_scalar = _kitspine.toml_value\n937\t\n938\t\n939\tdef set_process_key(dest, section, key, value, dry_run=False, add_if_missing=False):\n940\t    \"\"\"Set `[section] key = value` in `docs/process.toml`, IN PLACE.\n941\t\n942\t    A LINE REWRITE, not a re-serialization: stdlib has no TOML writer, and the\n943\t    file's explanatory header is most of its value (the same reason the legacy\n944\t    appliers kept the template's `#` lines). The one-`key = value`-per-line\n945\t    convention this file owes the git hooks is exactly what makes a line\n946\t    rewrite exact.\n947\t\n948\t    Returns `\"set\"` (written), `\"same\"` (already that value) or `\"missing\"`\n949\t    (no such file/section/key, nothing written). THREE states, not a bool: the\n950\t    MIGRATOR must be able to tell \"already correct\" from \"I could not write\n951\t    it\", because deleting a legacy file on the strength of a write that never\n952\t    happened destroys a declared policy and reports success. It did exactly\n953\t    that once, on a hand-trimmed process.toml.\n954\t\n955\t    `add_if_missing` appends the key (creating the `[section]` header when\n956\t    needed) instead of answering `\"missing\"` — what the migrator passes, so a\n957\t    conversion is TOTAL. The scaffold flags leave it off: there, an absent key\n958\t    means someone deleted it deliberately.\n959\t\n960\t    Implements: SR-138, LLR-156\n961\t    \"\"\"\n962\t    path = Path(dest) / PROCESS_TOML_REL\n963\t    if not path.is_file():\n964\t        return \"missing\"\n965\t    lines = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8-sig\").splitlines()\n966\t    want = \"{} = {}\".format(key, _toml_scalar(value))\n967\t    header = \"[{}]\".format(section)\n968\t    at, append_at = _locate_process_key(lines, header, key)\n969\t    if at is not None:\n970\t        if lines[at] == want:\n971\t            return \"same\"\n972\t        if not dry_run:\n973\t            lines[at] = want\n974\t            _write_text_lf(path, \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\")\n975\t        return \"set\"\n976\t    if not add_if_missing:\n977\t        return \"missing\"\n978\t    if not dry_run:\n979\t        if header in [ln.strip() for ln in lines]:\n980\t            lines.insert(append_at if append_at is not None else len(lines), want)\n981\t        else:\n982\t            lines.extend([\"\", header, want])\n983\t        _write_text_lf(path, \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\")\n984\t    return \"set\"\n985\t\n986\t\n987\tdef _locate_process_key(lines, header, key):\n988\t    \"\"\"`(index_of_key, index_to_append_at)` for `key` under `header`.\n989\t\n990\t    The pure SCAN half of `set_process_key`, split from the write half so\n991\t    neither sits at the C901 ceiling — and because \"where is it\" and \"what do I\n992\t    write\" are two questions. Either value is None when there is no answer.\"\"\"\n993\t    in_section, append_at = False, None\n994\t    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n995\t        stripped = line.strip()\n996\t        if stripped.startswith(\"[\") and stripped.endswith(\"]\"):\n997\t            in_section = stripped == header\n998\t            continue\n999\t        if not in_section:\n1000\t            continue\n1001\t        if \"=\" in stripped and stripped.split(\"=\", 1)[0].strip() == key:\n1002\t            return i, None\n1003\t        if stripped:\n1004\t            append_at = i + 1\n1005\t    return None, append_at\n1006\t\n1007\t\n1008\tdef apply_gate_policy(dest, level, dry_run):\n1009\t    \"\"\"Write a non-default gate-authority posture, as the THREE DIALS it is.\n1010\t\n1011\t    `--gate-policy` still takes the familiar word, because that is how the\n1012\t    posture is talked about — but the word is TRANSLATED here (SN-029,\n1013\t    `LEGACY_APPROVAL` above) rather than stored. Storing it was the\n1014\t    defect: the template ships `human_approval_through`, and\n1015\t    `approval_through` prefers that key, so a `gate_policy = \"autonomous\"`\n1016\t    written beside it was read by nothing and every repo that chose a\n1017\t    non-default posture scaffolded as fully attended — silently, since neither\n1018\t    dial is wrong on its own and `config_conflicts` had no rule against the\n1019\t    pair.\n1020\t\n1021\t    Also scaffolds the deviation-register skeleton (docs/gate-policy.md)\n1022\t    pre-filled for the level — prose, and still keyed by the word. Returns the\n1023\t    list of dest-relative paths written.\"\"\"\n1024\t    if level == \"attended\" or dry_run:\n1025\t        return []\n1026\t    for key, value in sorted(LEGACY_APPROVAL.get(level, {}).items()):\n1027\t        set_process_key(dest, \"attestation\", key, value)\n1028\t    register = dest / \"docs\" / \"gate-policy.md\"\n1029\t    written = [PROCESS_TOML_REL]\n1030\t    if not register.exists():\n1031\t        rows = \"\\n\".join(\n1032\t            \"| {} | {} | {} |\".format(*row) for row in GATE_POLICY_DEVIATIONS[level]\n1033\t        )\n1034\t        _write_text_lf(\n1035\t            register,\n1036\t            \"# Gate-authority deviation register — `{level}`\\n\\n\"\n1037\t            \"**Status:** DRAFT — approve with the owner, then keep in version \"\n1038\t            \"control.\\n\"\n1039\t            \"**What this is:** this repo declares the `{level}` gate authority \"\n1040\t            \"(`docs/process.toml` `[attestation] human_approval_through` \"\n1041\t            \"plus `keep_nondependent` and `final_review`; process.md \"\n1042\t            \"§4). \"\n1043\t            \"The kit-owned process doc is \"\n1044\t            \"never edited per-repo (a re-sync overwrites it); this register \"\n1045\t            'amends it (process-options.md \"Gate authority levels\"). Where '\n1046\t            \"the two disagree, this file wins — except the fixed points at \"\n1047\t            \"the bottom, which nothing overrides.\\n\\n\"\n1048\t            \"## Deviation register\\n\\n\"\n1049\t            \"| process.md clause | Standard behavior | This repo |\\n\"\n1050\t            \"|---|---|---|\\n\"\n1051\t            \"{rows}\\n\\n\"\n1052\t            \"{fixed}\".format(level=level, rows=rows, fixed=GATE_POLICY_FIXED_POINTS),\n1053\t        )\n1054\t        written.append(\"docs/gate-policy.md\")\n1055\t    return written\n1056\t\n1057\t\n1058\t# Who may publish (Thread 40, process-options.md \"Agent iteration branch &\n1059\t# sync\"). Declared once at scaffold time like the gate authority; the value is\n1060\t# honored by agent drivers/coordinators as a process rule (hooks are per-clone\n1061\t# and can only assist).\n1062\tPUSH_POLICY_CHOICES = (\"human\", \"agent-iteration\", \"agent\")\n1063\t\n1064\t\n1065\tdef apply_push_policy(dest, policy, dry_run):\n1066\t    \"\"\"Write a non-default push policy into `[policies] push` of\n1067\t    docs/process.toml (SN-028; same shape as apply_privacy_check).\"\"\"\n1068\t    if policy == \"human\" or dry_run:\n1069\t        return\n1070\t    set_process_key(dest, \"policies\", \"push\", policy)\n1071\t\n1072\t\n1073\tdef apply_privacy_check(dest, value, dry_run):\n1074\t    \"\"\"Write the privacy-check toggle into `[policies] privacy_check` of\n1075\t    docs/process.toml. Set at repo creation — an explicitly passed/answered\n1076\t    `true` overwrites the scaffolded default (`false`). `value` arrives as the\n1077\t    legacy one-word string; it is written as a TOML BOOLEAN, the one type the\n1078\t    gate reads as declared off (any other value reads ON).\"\"\"\n1079\t    if dry_run:\n1080\t        return\n1081\t    set_process_key(\n1082\t        dest, \"policies\", \"privacy_check\", str(value).strip().lower() == \"true\"\n1083\t    )\n1084\t\n1085\t\n1086\t# SN-029: the retired gate-authority enum, translated to the three dials that\n1087\t# replaced it. DUPLICATED from `agent_common.LEGACY_APPROVAL` under the F5\n1088\t# rule — bootstrap imports no kit sibling, because it is the one script an\n1089\t# adopter may run from a bare download before anything else exists — and pinned\n1090\t# equal by tests/test_rule_sync.py, which is how this kit keeps a duplicated\n1091\t# POLICY (as opposed to duplicated plumbing) from drifting.\n1092\t#\n1093\t# What each word meant, and why one key could not hold it: `attended` held every\n1094\t# tier and drained the station at an approval; `single-approve` held NO tier"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"4a020a9b-fc9f-41de-9bdc-c21e138c51df","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:52:23.369Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-966\\project-trajectory\\scripts\\bootstrap.py","content":"# producing a file `tomllib` refuses to read back.\n_toml_scalar = _kitspine.toml_value\n\n\ndef set_process_key(dest, section, key, value, dry_run=False, add_if_missing=False):\n    \"\"\"Set `[section] key = value` in `docs/process.toml`, IN PLACE.\n\n    A LINE REWRITE, not a re-serialization: stdlib has no TOML writer, and the\n    file's explanatory header is most of its value (the same reason the legacy\n    appliers kept the template's `#` lines). The one-`key = value`-per-line\n    convention this file owes the git hooks is exactly what makes a line\n    rewrite exact.\n\n    Returns `\"set\"` (written), `\"same\"` (already that value) or `\"missing\"`\n    (no such file/section/key, nothing written). THREE states, not a bool: the\n    MIGRATOR must be able to tell \"already correct\" from \"I could not write\n    it\", because deleting a legacy file on the strength of a write that never\n    happened destroys a declared policy and reports success. It did exactly\n    that once, on a hand-trimmed process.toml.\n\n    `add_if_missing` appends the key (creating the `[section]` header when\n    needed) instead of answering `\"missing\"` — what the migrator passes, so a\n    conversion is TOTAL. The scaffold flags leave it off: there, an absent key\n    means someone deleted it deliberately.\n\n    Implements: SR-138, LLR-156\n    \"\"\"\n    path = Path(dest) / PROCESS_TOML_REL\n    if not path.is_file():\n        return \"missing\"\n    lines = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8-sig\").splitlines()\n    want = \"{} = {}\".format(key, _toml_scalar(value))\n    header = \"[{}]\".format(section)\n    at, append_at = _locate_process_key(lines, header, key)\n    if at is not None:\n        if lines[at] == want:\n            return \"same\"\n        if not dry_run:\n            lines[at] = want\n            _write_text_lf(path, \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\")\n        return \"set\"\n    if not add_if_missing:\n        return \"missing\"\n    if not dry_run:\n        if header in [ln.strip() for ln in lines]:\n            lines.insert(append_at if append_at is not None else len(lines), want)\n        else:\n            lines.extend([\"\", header, want])\n        _write_text_lf(path, \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\")\n    return \"set\"\n\n\ndef _locate_process_key(lines, header, key):\n    \"\"\"`(index_of_key, index_to_append_at)` for `key` under `header`.\n\n    The pure SCAN half of `set_process_key`, split from the write half so\n    neither sits at the C901 ceiling — and because \"where is it\" and \"what do I\n    write\" are two questions. Either value is None when there is no answer.\"\"\"\n    in_section, append_at = False, None\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n        stripped = line.strip()\n        if stripped.startswith(\"[\") and stripped.endswith(\"]\"):\n            in_section = stripped == header\n            continue\n        if not in_section:\n            continue\n        if \"=\" in stripped and stripped.split(\"=\", 1)[0].strip() == key:\n            return i, None\n        if stripped:\n            append_at = i + 1\n    return None, append_at\n\n\ndef apply_gate_policy(dest, level, dry_run):\n    \"\"\"Write a non-default gate-authority posture, as the THREE DIALS it is.\n\n    `--gate-policy` still takes the familiar word, because that is how the\n    posture is talked about — but the word is TRANSLATED here (SN-029,\n    `LEGACY_APPROVAL` above) rather than stored. Storing it was the\n    defect: the template ships `human_approval_through`, and\n    `approval_through` prefers that key, so a `gate_policy = \"autonomous\"`\n    written beside it was read by nothing and every repo that chose a\n    non-default posture scaffolded as fully attended — silently, since neither\n    dial is wrong on its own and `config_conflicts` had no rule against the\n    pair.\n\n    Also scaffolds the deviation-register skeleton (docs/gate-policy.md)\n    pre-filled for the level — prose, and still keyed by the word. Returns the\n    list of dest-relative paths written.\"\"\"\n    if level == \"attended\" or dry_run:\n        return []\n    for key, value in sorted(LEGACY_APPROVAL.get(level, {}).items()):\n        set_process_key(dest, \"attestation\", key, value)\n    register = dest / \"docs\" / \"gate-policy.md\"\n    written = [PROCESS_TOML_REL]\n    if not register.exists():\n        rows = \"\\n\".join(\n            \"| {} | {} | {} |\".format(*row) for row in GATE_POLICY_DEVIATIONS[level]\n        )\n        _write_text_lf(\n            register,\n            \"# Gate-authority deviation register — `{level}`\\n\\n\"\n            \"**Status:** DRAFT — approve with the owner, then keep in version \"\n            \"control.\\n\"\n            \"**What this is:** this repo declares the `{level}` gate authority \"\n            \"(`docs/process.toml` `[attestation] human_approval_through` \"\n            \"plus `keep_nondependent` and `final_review`; process.md \"\n            \"§4). \"\n            \"The kit-owned process doc is \"\n            \"never edited per-repo (a re-sync overwri
